-Production uses $n=5$ iterations. Huang \emph{et al.}~\cite{Huang:2025omnifold} select 45 iterations for OmniFold-family methods (20 for their binned benchmark) using a binned $\chi^{2}$ against known mock-data truth, a criterion they note is infeasible on real data. We do not adopt that recommendation. Their mock data inject a large prior--data \emph{shape} discrepancy through an RPA-modified quasielastic model, requiring many iterations. Here the simulation prior differs from the measured distributions in shape as well as normalization: the data exceed it by about \fvval{ledger-datamc-pct}\%, and the data/MC ratio rises with $\pT$ from \fvval{ledger-datamc-lo} to about \fvval{ledger-datamc-hi} (ref.~\cite{AnalysisNote2026}, section~\ref*{N-sec:controlplots}). Cross-experiment production also typically uses ${\sim}5$ iterations~\cite{Canelli:2025guide}. The five-observable estimator's five iterations were instead chosen in simulation closure studies (section~\ref{supp:superseded}). For the two-dimensional unfoldings, two checks test the integrated cross section. For the exact-split cross-check, configuration~(b), the total cross section agrees to within \SI{\fvval{status-iter-pct}}{\percent} between five and ten iterations, and the two-dimensional result reproduces the integrated cross section of an independently published binned measurement of the same data (ref.~\cite{AnalysisNote2026}, section~\ref*{N-sec:results}). An integral does not test the shape's convergence. Between the same five and ten iterations, the per-bin shape of configuration~(b) differs by \SI{\fvval{status-iter-shape-rms}}{\percent} RMS (ref.~\cite{AnalysisNote2026}, section~\ref*{N-sec:validation}). In a purity-based LightGBM unfolding at one training seed, the $\chi^{2}/\mathrm{ndf}$ against the published result under the published covariance changes from \fvval{status-lgbm-it5} at five iterations to \fvval{status-lgbm-it10} at ten, less than it differs between learners (section~\ref{supp:su