-The two tunes are \fvval{f1-tune-g18} (\fvval{f1-tune-g18-short}) and \fvval{f1-tune-ar23} (\fvval{f1-tune-ar23-short}). Their pseudo-data use the half split and are unfolded with configuration~(e). Each tune's prediction is a GENIE~v3 sample of $\fvval{f1-nevents}$ events, binned on the fine five-observable cells. In fine cell $c$, the ratio to the GENIE~2.12 CV reference is $t_c=g_c/g^{\mathrm{ref}}_c$, the ratio of the two predictions' integrated cross sections. The ratio is gated on statistical precision. A cell keeps its own ratio when the combined relative statistical uncertainty of $g_c$ and $g^{\mathrm{ref}}_c$ is at most \fvval{f1-statmax}. The remaining cells of each coarse cell of the joint (J) grid share one pooled ratio when its relative uncertainty is at most \fvval{f1-statmax}, and otherwise take $t_c=1$. Only cells where the reported estimator's central value and both predictions are positive enter, and every other cell takes $t_c=1$.
+The two tunes are \fvval{f1-tune-g18} (\fvval{f1-tune-g18-short}) and \fvval{f1-tune-ar23} (\fvval{f1-tune-ar23-short}). Their pseudo-data use the half split and are unfolded with the five-observable estimator. Each tune's prediction is a GENIE~v3 sample of $\fvval{f1-nevents}$ events, binned on the fine five-observable cells. In fine cell $c$, the ratio to the GENIE~2.12 CV reference is $t_c=g_c/g^{\mathrm{ref}}_c$, the ratio of the two predictions' integrated cross sections. The ratio is gated on statistical precision. A cell keeps its own ratio when the combined relative statistical uncertainty of $g_c$ and $g^{\mathrm{ref}}_c$ is at most \fvval{f1-statmax}. The remaining cells of each coarse cell of the joint (J) grid share one pooled ratio when its relative uncertainty is at most \fvval{f1-statmax}, and otherwise take $t_c=1$. Only cells where the reported estimator's central value and both predictions are positive enter, and every other cell takes $t_c=1$.
 
 The ratio is then normalized, $\omega_c=k\,t_c$ on the assigned cells, with $k$ chosen so that the simulation's prior truth weight summed over those cells is unchanged, and it is applied to each simulated event by its fine cell. The pseudo-experiments rescale the reweighted in-grid total to the prior's by one constant. Each held-out tune therefore changes the shape only: it is a gated, normalized shape deformation of the prior. Table~\ref{tab:tune-gate} gives the gate's outcome.
